<!-- faultline:start -->
## Faultline adversarial engineering gate

For substantial new features, architecture changes, APIs/data models, authentication/authorization, integrations, migrations, bug fixes with broad impact, or major refactors:

1. Before designing specifications or implementing substantial changes, invoke `faultline-preflight` or `faultline-break-sweep`.
2. For an existing repository, brownfield reconnaissance is mandatory: inspect the actual repository, entry points, modules, dependencies, data/state, runtime, configuration, tests, CI, deployment, observability, and recovery boundaries.
3. Trace concrete code paths and side effects. Identify current behavior/contracts that must remain true and all affected callers/consumers.
4. Try to falsify the actual implementation: inspect test scripts, run safe focused checks when permitted, model duplicate/retry/concurrency/interruption/partial-commit paths, and clearly separate executed tests from proposed tests.
5. Claude Code can leverage specialist subagents configured in `.claude/agents/` (or run sequential passes if subagents are disabled). Disclose execution context.
6. Keep preflight read-only. Do not change production code, migrations, config, tests, or deployment files.
7. Save reports under `docs/engineering/pre-spec/` for proposed changes or `docs/engineering/faultline/` for general existing-code audits.
8. After a specification or proposal is drafted, run `faultline-spec-audit` before implementation to verify risk traceability.
9. Do not claim exhaustive coverage. Record inspected scope, evidence, uninspected areas, commands actually run, uncertainty, agent provenance, and blind spots.

Faultline is an analysis workflow, not a permission boundary. Host permissions and CI are required for hard enforcement.
<!-- faultline:end -->
